// #pragma once

// THE ONE PLACE AN EQUIRECTANGULAR SKY IS READ, and the one place an HDR skybox's authored look is
// applied.
//
// WHY THIS IS A SHARED TEXT AND NOT TWO COPIES. The HDR environment is built from ONE panorama by TWO
// compute programs that must agree pixel for pixel about what that panorama says in a direction:
// PanoramaToCubemap writes the radiance cube the background is drawn from and the GGX prefilter
// convolves, and DiffuseIrradiance integrates the SAME panorama into the irradiance cube every lit
// surface reads. Until this header existed both carried their own copy of the three-line
// direction->UV mapping. Two copies of one mapping is how an authored rotation reaches the background
// and misses the lighting — the middle-link-drops-a-property shape this engine has paid for repeatedly
// — and the copies cannot be kept honest by review, because each one reads correct on its own.
//
// WHY THE LOOK IS APPLIED HERE AND NOT AT THE SAMPLE SITES IN THE PBR SHADERS. Rotating or tinting the
// sky at sample time would mean threading a uniform through five programs that read the environment
// (the deferred composite and four forward PBR variants), each with its own binding layout — five
// chances for one of them to miss it, and every one of them a descriptor change. Applied at the bake,
// the transform is in the single image that every consumer descends from, so a consumer CANNOT miss it.
// The price is that a change costs a rebake rather than a frame; that is the same price this engine
// already pays for moving the procedural sky's sun, and it is measured in SkyboxRenderer's bake log.

// The panorama texel a world DIRECTION reads, with the sky's authored yaw applied.
//
// @p yawCosSin is (cos yaw, sin yaw) — the angle is passed pre-evaluated because it is constant over
// the whole dispatch and a transcendental per texel of a 1024-face cube is six million of them.
//
// THE ROTATION IS APPLIED TO THE LOOKUP, BY MINUS THE ANGLE, so the IMAGE turns by plus the angle: an
// author who types 90 wants the sun that was in the east to end up in the north, not the camera to have
// turned under a fixed sky. The relation is asserted rather than described —
// Desert/Tests/Engine/SkyPanorama compiles this text as C++ and checks that rotating a direction by
// +yaw and then looking it up returns the texel the unrotated direction used to read.
vec2 PanoramaSampleUV(vec3 direction, vec2 yawCosSin)
{
    const float kPi = 3.14159265358979323846;

    // Rotation about +Y (up) by -yaw. Written out rather than built as a mat3 because this text also
    // compiles as C++, where a mat3 literal's column/row order is one more thing to get wrong.
    vec3 d = vec3( yawCosSin.x * direction.x + yawCosSin.y * direction.z,
                   direction.y,
                   yawCosSin.x * direction.z - yawCosSin.y * direction.x );

    float phi   = atan( d.z, d.x );
    // CLAMPED: a direction that arrives a bit longer than unit (the cube-face vectors are normalized,
    // but a caller's need not be) makes acos return NaN, and one NaN texel poisons every mip the
    // prefilter builds from it.
    float theta = acos( clamp( d.y, -1.0, 1.0 ) );

    return vec2( phi / ( 2.0 * kPi ) + 0.5, theta / kPi );
}

// The authored brightness and colour of the sky, applied to radiance read out of the panorama.
//
// ONE LINE, AND IT IS A FUNCTION ON PURPOSE. Both compute programs must apply it, and a bare multiply
// is the kind of thing that gets added to one of them; a named call is something a source-text census
// can require of both (Desert/Tests/Engine/SkyPanorama). `gain` is tint * intensity, combined by the
// caller so the shader has one number and the component keeps two knobs.
vec3 ApplySkyGain(vec3 radiance, vec3 gain)
{
    return radiance * gain;
}
